q_dac551 The missed mechanism was that at the ultra-low SGD rate (3e-5), LayerNorm is more important than either residual connections or ReLU’s activation advantage. Thus D (one LN, non-residual SiLU) beats B (zero LN, residual ReLU), reversing my choice. K1006’s “LN count first” principle was helpful, but I underweighted it; K1039 misled me because its SGD regime starts around 1e-4 and does not cover this very-low-step regime. In the corpus, two directly comparable multivariate SGD/3e-5 comparisons both favored the more-LN architecture, including a non-residual+LN model decisively beating residual+zero-LN.
q_9e42d2 Choice A won because the smooth but interaction-rich symbolic target benefits from a sufficiently deep SiLU MLP paired with a practical AdamW learning rate. Choice B’s Adagrad at 0.0003 likely made slower progress, while Choice C’s 3e-05 AdamW rate effectively undertrained the model within the fixed 32,768-sample budget. No KB claims were used.
q_06b6b2 I over-weighted residual gradient flow and under-weighted A’s repeated late normalization: LayerNorm can amplify weak, input-dependent features after non-residual contractions, so depth need not imply slower learning. This is a plausible explanation for A’s win, not an established causal result. No KB claims were consulted. Backtesting found no closely matched corpus comparisons; a relaxed lab comparison had only two finite pairs, split 1–1. A controlled AdamW 3e-5 experiment found late-normalized non-residual MSE 1.476, versus 1.128 for an all-normalized residual variant; early-only and no-normalization variants returned nonfi

